<div class="article-body">

<p>Fungus gnats are not a bug problem. They are a watering problem. If you have already tried the sprays and the sticky traps, you probably watched the little black cloud come back about two weeks later. That is not you failing. That is the life cycle.</p>

<p>I found out the slow way. Four trays of microgreens on a kitchen shelf, a light strip overhead, and one morning a drift of tiny black flies around my coffee cup. I assumed it was mold gone airborne. It was not. It was gnat larvae chewing the root hairs off plants I had spent 11 days growing.</p>

<p>So here is the short version, so you do not lose four trays like I did: <strong>dry out the top inch of your growing medium, move the air, and run one drench on a schedule instead of once.</strong> Do that and the gnats are gone in about 3 weeks. Every other fix I tried was treating a stage I could see while the stage I could not see kept breeding.</p>

<h2 id="what">1. What Fungus Gnats Actually Are (and What They Are Not)</h2>

<p>Fungus gnats are small flies in the family Sciaridae. The adults are about 1/8 inch long, dark brown or black, with see-through wings folded flat on their backs. If you catch one under a magnifier, the wing has a rounded Y-shaped vein near the tip. That Y is the easiest way to tell them apart from fruit flies.</p>

<p>The larvae are what actually matter. Pale maggots about 1/4 inch long with a shiny black head, living in the top 2 to 3 inches of your medium. They feed on fungi and decaying organic matter. When there are enough of them, they move on to the root hairs and tunnel into young roots.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> the flies you see are not eating your plants. The larvae are. Adults do not bite, do not spread disease, and do not feed on plants at all. They are a nuisance that lands in your drink. The larvae are the reason your seedlings stall.</p>

<p>Two fast ways to tell them apart from fruit flies. Fruit flies come for ripe fruit and sugar, so a vinegar trap catches them. Fungus gnats ignore vinegar traps completely. And gnat larvae come up for a slice of raw potato, which makes a potato slice the cheapest test there is. Leave one on the surface for 3 or 4 days and lift it. If you see maggots, you have your answer.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Where They Actually Come From</div>
  <p>Usually the bag of potting mix you just opened, or a plant that spent the summer outside and came back in for fall. The eggs were already there. The wet surface is what lets them multiply. You did not bring a pest home. You brought home a surface it likes.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="cycle">2. The 3-Week Life Cycle That Explains Every Failed Fix</h2>

<p><strong>Pay attention to this number: 200.</strong> A single female lays up to 200 eggs, tucked into the cracks and crevices of the moist surface of your medium. Not deep. The surface.</p>

<p>Here is the timeline, straight from extension entomology. Eggs hatch in 3 to 6 days. The larvae go through four stages over 1 to 2 weeks. Then 4 to 5 days as pupae near the surface. Then adults emerge and live about a week. Egg to adult takes 3 to 4 weeks when conditions are good.</p>

<p>Now the part that no quick fix accounts for. Indoors, the generations overlap. On any given day your tray holds eggs, larvae, pupae, and adults at the same time. Which means one treatment kills the generation that is vulnerable today and does nothing to the eggs already sitting in the surface. Seven to ten days later, the next generation hatches and you are back where you started.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The Timing Math</div>
  <p>One application removes one generation. There are four overlapping on your shelf right now. To cover 4 weeks of hatching you need 3 to 4 applications spaced 5 to 7 days apart. Most people apply once, see the flies drop, and stop. That is the whole trick.</p>
</div>

<p>This is also why the infestation looks like it appears overnight. Imagine a tray that looks clean for 3 weeks and then explodes. Nothing exploded. The first generation just finished growing up all at once.</p>

<h2 id="why">3. Why Peroxide and Sticky Traps Alone Do Not Work</h2>

<p>Every popular fix attacks a stage you can see. That is the pattern.</p>

<p><strong>Hydrogen peroxide.</strong> A mix of 1 part 3% hydrogen peroxide to 4 parts water does kill larvae on contact. But only the larvae in the layer it soaks into, and only the ones that are there at that moment. It leaves eggs and pupae untouched, and it does not keep working. Some extension folks also point out that it knocks back the beneficial microbes in your medium, the same ones breaking down organic matter for you. So it is a real tool and also an incomplete one. If you want it in the cabinet anyway, <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=food+grade+hydrogen+peroxide+3+percent&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">3% hydrogen peroxide</a> is what you want, not the stronger stuff.</p>

<p><strong>Sticky traps.</strong> Yellow cards catch adults. Useful for counting and for watching the trend, but catching 40 flies means nothing if 400 eggs are already in the surface. And vinegar traps, the ones you use for fruit flies, catch zero fungus gnats. I know because I left one out for 10 days.</p>

<p><strong>Diatomaceous earth.</strong> It works by scratching insects that crawl across it, and only while it is bone dry. The moment your medium surface is damp, which it will be, it stops cutting. So <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=diatomaceous+earth+food+grade&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">food grade diatomaceous earth</a> is fine on a dry shelf and close to useless on a wet one.</p>

<p><strong>Here is why that matters:</strong> none of these are wrong. They are just aimed at the adults and the top larvae, while the problem lives in the eggs, the pupae, and the wet surface you refresh every time you water. Fix the surface and the tools above start working with you instead of against you.</p>

<h2 id="fix">4. The Fix: Dry the Top Inch, Move the Air, Schedule One Drench</h2>

<p>Three parts. Skipping any one of them is how this drags on for two months.</p>

<p><strong>Part 1: the surface.</strong> Let the top inch of medium dry out between waterings. If your plants can take it, water from the bottom. If you are growing microgreens or seedlings in shallow trays and cannot bottom water, top-dress with about 1/2 inch of coarse sand or fine gravel. It keeps the surface dry and stops the surface being a good place to lay eggs.</p>

<p><strong>Part 2: airflow.</strong> A small fan moving air across the trays does two things. It dries the surface between waterings, and it makes it hard for the adults to settle and lay. I had read this a dozen times and skipped it for a month because it sounded like a detail. It is not a detail. It was the single change that made the difference on my shelf. One honest note: the fan does not replace your light timer. If your trays sit under strips like mine, keep the same hours and just add moving air. The exact strips and light specs I run are in the <a href="/grow-lights/barrina-t5-4pack">grow light database</a> if you want to compare setups.</p>

<p><strong>Part 3: the drench on a schedule.</strong> This is what actually breaks the cycle. Use a Bti product, the same bacteria sold as mosquito dunks for ponds. It kills larvae when they eat it, and it does not touch eggs, pupae, or adults. So you apply, wait, apply again. Every 5 to 7 days, for 3 to 4 rounds. Mix it into your watering can and water it in properly so it reaches the top 2 to 3 inches where the larvae are. Scattering it dry on the surface does almost nothing.</p>

<p>I am not a scientist. I am a guy with a shelf in his kitchen and a bad habit of buying things before reading the instructions. But once I understood this as a 4-week calendar problem instead of a spray problem, it stopped coming back. That was the whole shift.</p>

<p>One more thing, because everyone asks. <strong>Do they just go away on their own?</strong> Sometimes, if you let the medium dry out and stop watering for a while. Usually not, because the next watering restarts the clock. And they are not harmful to you. They do not bite and they do not spread disease. Their damage is to seedlings, cuttings, and any plant young enough that losing root hairs is a real hit. In pure water systems like Kratky or DWC with clay pebbles you will rarely see them at all. They need damp organic material to breed, which is why seed starting mix is their favorite room in the house.</p>

<p>If your trays are the shallow microgreen kind with a wet mat and no airflow, you are running the setup gnats love most. I wrote about the airflow side of that in <a href="/posts/how-to-prevent-mold-on-microgreens">mold on microgreens</a>, and the same fan solves both problems. If you are starting seeds indoors right now, the mix itself is usually the source, and my <a href="/posts/how-to-start-seeds-indoors">seed starting setup</a> covers how I keep that surface dry without slowing germination.</p>

<p>I thought I had it figured out. I bought the sticky traps, I bought the Bti, I drenched the whole shelf, and for two weeks I felt smug about it.</p>

<p>Then they came back. It started with 4 trays of microgreens. Then a 72 cell tray of basil. Then I found flies in every pot on the shelf. Not because the fix failed, but because I had followed the label instead of the calendar. The bag says one application. The life cycle says three to four. <strong>This is where it gets interesting:</strong> I had wiped out one generation out of four, watched the flies drop, and declared victory during the quiet week before the next hatch. The gnats did not beat me. My sense of timing did.</p>

<h2 id="choose">5. Three Ways to Handle This (and What I Would Pick)</h2>

<p>So you know what you are actually fighting now. That leaves a decision about how to fight it.</p>

<p><strong>The trap: sticky cards and nothing else.</strong> About $8 for a pack of <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=yellow+sticky+traps+fungus+gnats&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">yellow sticky traps</a>. You will catch a lot of adults, it will feel like progress, and in about two weeks the numbers come right back. This is what most people do because it is visible. It is a scoreboard, not a fix.</p>

<p><strong>The overkill: scorched earth.</strong> Throw out every pot and every open bag of medium, repot everything, start clean. It works. It also costs a weekend and roughly $80 in fresh medium, and if one new bag carries eggs, you are back on day one. I have done this and I do not recommend it.</p>

<p><strong>The boring one that works.</strong> Dry surface, airflow, and <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mosquito+bits&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">mosquito bits</a> on a 5 to 7 day schedule for four rounds. Add a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=coarse+horticultural+sand+top+dressing&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">coarse sand top dressing</a> if your surface will not stay dry, and a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=small+clip+on+fan+grow+tent&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">small clip fan</a> for the shelf. Total is about $35. It takes 3 to 4 weeks and it ends.</p>

<h2 id="honest">6. Here Is the Honest Truth</h2>

<p><strong>Here is the honest truth:</strong> this method is boring and it takes 3 weeks. Nothing here kills every gnat today. You will still see adults for two or three weeks, and that is normal, not failure. If you want a same day wipeout, you will not get one from any product on this list. The gnats die on a 4-week schedule and you do not get to move it.</p>

<p>Two more things I would want to know before buying. The <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mosquito+bits&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">mosquito bits</a> smell like a pond for a day after you mix them, and if you scatter them across a wet surface and leave them there, they can grow a layer of surface fuzz. Mix them into a watering can instead and that goes away. And the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=coarse+horticultural+sand+top+dressing&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">coarse sand</a> top dressing makes your medium look like a zen garden, which some people hate. In shallow microgreen trays you may not be able to use it at all. That is fine. The fan and the drench carry most of the work.</p>

<p>Here is why the fan earns its place: it is the cheapest item on the list, it fixes the surface, and it costs you no plants to try. If I could keep only one thing, it would be the $12 fan.</p>

<p>If you are in the middle of this right now, here is the order I would buy in. Put the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=small+clip+on+fan+grow+tent&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">small clip fan</a> on the shelf first, because airflow is what makes the rest work. Then grab the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=yellow+sticky+traps+fungus+gnats&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">yellow sticky traps</a> so you have a number to watch each week. Then the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mosquito+bits&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">mosquito bits</a>, drenched on schedule, not once.</p>

<p>The bits were about $15 when I last checked, but they bounce between $12 and $18. If they are under $16, that is a solid deal. Everything else is small money. Let my four lost trays pay for your lesson instead of your own.</p>

